  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday morning through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 228 PM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nThe long term begins as the longwave upper-level trough retreats \ntoward the northeast, leaving the region in a general ridging \npattern through the extended period. This will reinvigorate our \nsummertime conditions, with high temperatures climbing back into the \nlow to mid-90s for most of our CWA by midweek. These rising \ntemperatures, combined with an ongoing influx of low-level moisture \nfrom the Gulf, will keep dewpoint temperatures in the low to mid-70s \nmaking it feel downright unpleasant during the afternoons. For the \ntime being, this combination will remain below Heat Advisory levels, \nbut we will need to monitor the trends into early next week as \nthings heat up. \n\nThis pattern will provide for diurnally driven isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms each day. However, the prospect of severe weather \nremains low in the absence of noteworthy shear or more supportive \ndynamics. Regardless, daily afternoon instability will provide the \npotential for a few strong storms to briefly become severe, with \nstrong gusty winds, frequent lightning, and heavy rainfall. The \nbottom line is to expect more of the same August weather, with no \nreal change in the pattern through at least the end of next week.",
